<!-- ===== SECTION 1: RAG FUNDAMENTALS REVISITED ===== -->
<section class="section reveal" id="rag-fundamentals">
  <span class="section-label">Core Concepts</span>
  <h2>RAG System Fundamentals</h2>

  <p>Retrieval-Augmented Generation combines an information retriever with a generative LLM to ground model outputs in external documents. Rather than relying solely on training data (which becomes stale), RAG systems fetch relevant context dynamically and feed it to the LLM alongside the query.</p>

  <p>The fundamental insight is elegant: <em>providing relevant context before generation dramatically improves accuracy, reduces hallucination, and enables fact verification</em>. However, implementing RAG effectively requires careful orchestration of retrieval quality, latency, cost, and generation capability.</p>

  <!-- ANALOGY BOX -->
  <div class="analogy">
    <h4>💡 Think of It This Way</h4>
    <p>Imagine a student in an open-book exam. Without RAG, the student relies entirely on memory. With RAG, they can reference course materials during the exam. Final answer quality depends on three things: (1) efficiently finding the right references, (2) references being complete and accurate, and (3) skillfully synthesizing references into a coherent response. A poor student with great references beats a genius student without them.</p>
  </div>

