  <p>A RAG system consists of four stages: (1) <em>Query Encoding</em> — converting user input into an embedding representation, (2) <em>Retrieval</em> — finding similar documents in a vector database, (3) <em>Optional Reranking</em> — refining results with a more expensive but accurate model, and (4) <em>Generation</em> — using the LLM to synthesize a final answer from context and query.</p>

  <!-- WHY THIS MATTERS -->
  <div class="why-matters">
    <h4>🌍 Why This Matters</h4>
    <p><strong>In Financial AI:</strong> Investment research systems using RAG must retrieve and cite sources. A hedge fund's RAG for market analysis needs high retrieval quality so analysts can verify recommendations against original sources. Poor retrieval loses client trust and exposes the firm to regulatory risk.</p>
    <p><strong>In Life Sciences AI:</strong> Clinical decision support systems require accurate medical literature retrieval. An outdated or irrelevant treatment protocol retrieved by RAG could influence patient care. Reranking and quality assurance become critical safety mechanisms.</p>
  </div>

  <!-- KEY TAKEAWAY -->
  <div class="key-takeaway">
    <h4>✦ Key Takeaways</h4>
    <ul>
      <li>RAG grounds LLM outputs in external documents, reducing hallucination and enabling fact verification</li>
      <li>Four stages: query encoding, retrieval, optional reranking, and generation</li>
      <li>Quality depends on retrieval relevance, context completeness, and LLM's ability to synthesize information</li>
      <li>Production systems often add reranking, metadata filtering, and adaptive strategies</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <!-- QUIZ -->
  <div class="quiz-box">
