<!-- ===== SECTION 2: DENSE vs SPARSE RETRIEVAL ===== -->
<section class="section reveal" id="dense-vs-sparse">
  <span class="section-label">Architecture Patterns</span>
  <h2>Dense vs. Sparse Retrieval Trade-offs</h2>

  <p>Two retrieval paradigms dominate production systems: <em>dense retrieval</em> (neural embeddings) and <em>sparse retrieval</em> (term-based methods like BM25). Each has different strengths, and the best systems often combine both.</p>

  <h3>Dense Retrieval: Semantic Understanding</h3>
  <p>Dense retrieval encodes queries and documents into continuous vector embeddings. This excels at capturing semantic similarity — "What is France's capital?" and "Which city leads France administratively?" are semantically similar even with minimal word overlap. A good embedding model bridges this semantic gap.</p>

  <div class="code-header"><span class="code-lang">Python</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div>
  <pre><code><span class="cm"># Dense retrieval with embeddings</span>
<span class="im">from</span> <span class="dt">sentence_transformers</span> <span class="im">import</span> <span class="dt">SentenceTransformer</span>
<span class="im">import</span> <span class="dt">numpy</span> <span class="kw">as</span> <span class="dt">np</span>

<span class="cm"># Load pre-trained embedding model</span>
<span class="va">model</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">SentenceTransformer</span>(<span class="st">"all-MiniLM-L6-v2"</span>)

<span class="va">docs</span> <span class="op">=</span> [<span class="st">"Paris is capital of France"</span>,
         <span class="st">"Machine learning powers AI"</span>,
         <span class="st">"Embeddings capture meaning"</span>]

<span class="cm"># One-time encoding at indexing</span>
<span class="va">doc_vecs</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">model</span>.<span class="fn">encode</span>(<span class="va">docs</span>)

<span class="cm"># Query time: encode and search</span>
<span class="va">query</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="st">"Which city governs France?"</span>
<span class="va">q_vec</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">model</span>.<span class="fn">encode</span>(<span class="va">query</span>)
<span class="va">scores</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">np</span>.<span class="fn">dot</span>(<span class="va">q_vec</span>, <span class="va">doc_vecs</span>.<span class="fn">T</span>)
<span class="va">top_idx</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">np</span>.<span class="fn">argsort</span>(<span class="va">scores</span>)[::<span class="op">-</span><span class="nm">1</span>][<span class="nm">0</span>]
<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="st">f"Best: </span>{<span class="va">docs</span>[<span class="va">top_idx</span>]}<span class="st">"</span>)</code></pre>
  <div class="code-explain">
    <p><strong>What is happening:</strong> We load a sentence embedding model and encode documents into dense vectors. At query time, we encode the query and compute dot-product similarity with all document vectors. This scales to millions with proper indexing (FAISS, HNSW).</p>
  </div>

  <h3>Sparse Retrieval: Exact Matching</h3>
  <p>Sparse retrieval (BM25, TF-IDF) treats documents as bags of words and matches on term overlap. "Machine Learning" matches documents containing those exact terms. Fast, interpretable, and excellent for named entities and precise terminology.</p>

  <div class="code-header"><span class="code-lang">Python</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div>
  <pre><code><span class="cm"># Sparse retrieval using BM25</span>
<span class="im">from</span> <span class="dt">rank_bm25</span> <span class="im">import</span> <span class="dt">BM25Okapi</span>

<span class="va">docs</span> <span class="op">=</span> [<span class="st">"quick brown fox jumps"</span>,
       <span class="st">"clever fox is smart"</span>,
       <span class="st">"loyal dogs are companions"</span>]

<span class="cm"># Tokenize and build index (single pass)</span>
<span class="va">tok_docs</span> <span class="op">=</span> [<span class="va">d</span>.<span class="fn">lower</span>().<span class="fn">split</span>() <span class="kw">for</span> <span class="va">d</span> <span class="kw">in</span> <span class="va">docs</span>]
<span class="va">bm25</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">BM25Okapi</span>(<span class="va">tok_docs</span>)

<span class="cm"># Score query efficiently</span>
<span class="va">query</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="st">"fox animal"</span>
<span class="va">scores</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">bm25</span>.<span class="fn">get_scores</span>(<span class="va">query</span>.<span class="fn">lower</span>().<span class="fn">split</span>())
<span class="va">best_idx</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">max</span>(<span class="fn">range</span>(<span class="fn">len</span>(<span class="va">scores</span>)),
                    <span class="va">key</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="va">scores</span>.<span class="fn">__getitem__</span>)
<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="st">f"Match: </span>{<span class="va">docs</span>[<span class="va">best_idx</span>]}<span class="st">"</span>)</code></pre>
  <div class="code-explain">
    <p><strong>What is happening:</strong> BM25 builds an inverted index over document tokens. Query scoring is linear in vocabulary size (typically <100ms even for millions of docs). Transparent, fast, but weak on semantic paraphrase.</p>
  </div>

  <!-- INFOGRAPHIC 2: Dense vs Sparse Comparison -->
  <div class="infographic reveal">
    <div class="infographic-title">Dense vs. Sparse Retrieval: Complete Comparison</div>
    <div class="compare-grid">
      <div class="compare-card">
        <h5>Dense (Embeddings)</h5>
        <p><strong>Strengths:</strong> Semantic understanding, paraphrase tolerance, multilingual, context-aware</p>
        <p><strong>Weaknesses:</strong> Slow without acceleration, embedding quality dependent, harder to debug, requires vector DB</p>
        <p><strong>Latency:</strong> 50-500ms (depends on index size)</p>
        <p><strong>Best for:</strong> Semantic search, complex reasoning, cross-lingual queries</p>
      </div>
      <div class="compare-card">
        <h5>Sparse (BM25)</h5>
        <p><strong>Strengths:</strong> Fast, interpretable, domain terms, exact phrase match, no models needed</p>
        <p><strong>Weaknesses:</strong> Poor paraphrase handling, weak semantics, vocabulary gaps, no multilingual support</p>
        <p><strong>Latency:</strong> 1-10ms (extremely fast)</p>
        <p><strong>Best for:</strong> Technical docs, entity names, exact phrases, quick baselines</p>
      </div>
      <div class="compare-card">
        <h5>Hybrid (Both)</h5>
        <p><strong>Approach:</strong> Run both retrievers, fuse results using RRF (Reciprocal Rank Fusion) or learned models</p>
        <p><strong>Result:</strong> Best of both — semantic + exact match, more robust, handles edge cases better</p>
        <p><strong>Latency:</strong> 50-510ms (combined)</p>
        <p><strong>Outcome:</strong> Significantly better quality, modest latency overhead</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p>The choice between dense and sparse depends on your data and queries. Technical domains (medical, legal) favor sparse retrieval. Customer support with varied phrasing needs dense. Most production systems use hybrid: run both and combine results with Reciprocal Rank Fusion or learned fusion models.</p>

  <div class="key-takeaway">
    <h4>✦ Key Takeaways</h4>
    <ul>
      <li>Dense retrieval excels at semantic similarity but requires good embeddings and indexing</li>
      <li>Sparse retrieval is lightning-fast and interpretable but struggles with paraphrases</li>
      <li>Hybrid approaches combine both and typically outperform either alone</li>
      <li>Your choice should be driven by query characteristics, not convenience</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</section>

