<!-- ===== SECTION 3: ADVANCED PATTERNS ===== -->
<section class="section reveal" id="advanced-patterns">
  <span class="section-label">Advanced Architecture</span>
  <h2>Advanced RAG Patterns for Production</h2>

  <p>Beyond basic dense or sparse retrieval, production RAG systems employ sophisticated patterns to improve both quality and efficiency. Understanding these patterns helps you architect systems that scale.</p>

  <h3>Multi-Stage Ranking (Cascade Pattern)</h3>
  <p>Retrieve a large candidate set quickly using an efficient retriever, then rerank with a slower but more accurate model. This minimizes overall latency while improving quality. Stage 1 (BM25) returns top-100 in 5ms. Stage 2 (neural reranker) narrows to top-10 in 100ms. Stage 3 (LLM) generates with best context in 500ms. Total: ~600ms with excellent quality.</p>

  <h3>Adaptive RAG</h3>
  <p>Route queries to different strategies based on characteristics. Simple factual queries bypass retrieval entirely. Complex reasoning queries trigger multi-hop retrieval with iterative refinement. This adapts system behavior to query difficulty, optimizing latency.</p>

  <h3>Self-Filtering (Iterative Refinement)</h3>
  <p>Have the LLM decide if retrieved documents are relevant. If not, the LLM explicitly requests additional retrieval with modified queries. This creates a feedback loop until sufficient context is found — powerful for complex questions requiring multiple hops.</p>

