  <p>The cascade pattern is the workhorse of production RAG. It's simple to understand, easy to debug, and delivers excellent quality-to-latency ratio. The key insight is that you don't need a perfect retriever — you just need one that recalls the right documents in top-100. Reranking solves ranking accuracy.</p>

  <div class="code-header"><span class="code-lang">Python</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div>
  <pre><code><span class="cm"># Multi-stage RAG implementation</span>
<span class="im">from</span> <span class="dt">rank_bm25</span> <span class="im">import</span> <span class="dt">BM25Okapi</span>
<span class="im">from</span> <span class="dt">sentence_transformers</span> <span class="im">import</span> <span class="dt">CrossEncoder</span>

<span class="cm"># Stage 1: Fast BM25 retriever</span>
<span class="va">retriever</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">BM25Okapi</span>(<span class="va">tokenized_docs</span>)
<span class="va">top_100_docs</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">retriever</span>.<span class="fn">get_top_n</span>(
    <span class="va">query_tokens</span>, <span class="va">documents</span>, <span class="nm">100</span>
)

<span class="cm"># Stage 2: Neural reranker</span>
<span class="va">reranker</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">CrossEncoder</span>(
    <span class="st">"cross-encoder/ms-marco-MiniLM-L-12-v2"</span>
)
<span class="va">pairs</span> <span class="op">=</span> [[<span class="va">query</span>, <span class="va">doc</span>] <span class="kw">for</span> <span class="va">doc</span> <span class="kw">in</span> <span class="va">top_100_docs</span>]
<span class="va">scores</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">reranker</span>.<span class="fn">predict</span>(<span class="va">pairs</span>)

<span class="cm"># Get top-10 reranked docs</span>
<span class="va">top_10</span> <span class="op">=</span> [<span class="va">doc</span> <span class="kw">for</span> <span class="va">doc</span>, <span class="va">_</span> <span class="kw">in</span> <span class="fn">sorted</span>(
    <span class="fn">zip</span>(<span class="va">top_100_docs</span>, <span class="va">scores</span>),
    <span class="va">key</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="kw">lambda</span> <span class="va">x</span>: <span class="va">x</span>[<span class="nm">1</span>], <span class="va">reverse</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="kw">True</span>
)][:<span class="nm">10</span>]

<span class="cm"># Stage 3: Generate with best context</span>
<span class="va">context</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="st">"\n\n"</span>.<span class="fn">join</span>(<span class="va">top_10</span>)
<span class="va">prompt</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="st">f"""Answer the question using this context:

Context: {context}

Question: {query}"""</span>
<span class="va">answer</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">llm</span>.<span class="fn">generate</span>(<span class="va">prompt</span>)</code></pre>
  <div class="code-explain">
    <p><strong>What is happening:</strong> A classic three-stage pipeline. (1) BM25 quickly retrieves 100 candidates, (2) cross-encoder reranks to top-10 with neural scoring, (3) LLM generates the answer using only the best documents. This is how most production RAG systems work.</p>
  </div>

