<!-- ===== SECTION 4: PRODUCTION DEPLOYMENT ===== -->
<section class="section reveal" id="production">
  <span class="section-label">Production Design</span>
  <h2>Production Deployment Considerations</h2>

  <p>Moving RAG from prototype to production requires critical architectural decisions around scalability, reliability, and cost. Getting these wrong can sink your system.</p>

  <h3>Indexing Strategy</h3>
  <p>Flat indices are simple but slow (linear scan). Approximate Nearest Neighbor (ANN) indices like HNSW, IVF, or learned indices are fast but have accuracy trade-offs. Vector databases (Pinecone, Weaviate, Qdrant) abstract this complexity and handle distributed indexing.</p>

  <h3>Latency vs. Quality Trade-offs</h3>
  <p>Reranking improves quality significantly but costs latency. If you have a 50ms SLA, skip reranking. If quality is critical and users tolerate 1s latency, reranking is essential. Define your SLA first, then architect accordingly. Most systems target 200-500ms latency.</p>

  <h3>Cost Optimization</h3>
  <p>LLM inference dominates costs. Effective RAG reduces this by providing context that lets the LLM give shorter, more accurate responses. However, retrieval costs add up. Consider: caching frequent queries, batching inference, using smaller embedding models for initial retrieval, and tiered architectures (fast path for simple queries).</p>

