  <h3>Decision Criteria</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Latency SLA:</strong> What is your acceptable response time? &lt;100ms demands sparse only; 200-500ms enables hybrid; &gt;1s enables reranking</li>
    <li><strong>Quality Target:</strong> What metric matters most? Recall for comprehensiveness? Precision for accuracy? F1 for balance?</li>
    <li><strong>Dataset Size:</strong> Millions of docs need efficient indexing (HNSW). Thousands tolerate flat indices</li>
    <li><strong>Update Frequency:</strong> Frequent updates favor systems with easy re-indexing (sparse). Rare updates tolerate expensive processes</li>
    <li><strong>Cost Budget:</strong> Limited budget favors sparse. More generous enables multi-stage with reranking</li>
    <li><strong>Explainability:</strong> Need to justify results? Sparse retrieval is more interpretable than dense</li>
  </ul>

  <p>Start simple: baseline with BM25, measure, then optimize. Most projects find that hybrid retrieval or simple multi-stage addresses 90% of requirements while keeping implementation manageable.</p>

  <div class="key-takeaway">
    <h4>✦ Key Takeaways</h4>
    <ul>
      <li>No universal best RAG architecture — it depends on your constraints</li>
      <li>Start simple (BM25 or basic dense), measure quality, then optimize</li>
      <li>Profile bottlenecks: often LLM latency dominates, not retrieval</li>
      <li>Most production systems use hybrid or multi-stage approaches</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</section>

